# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
EN = {}
EN[99] = "Vakula meanwhile, having run several streets, stopped to catch his breath. <<VAKULA>>“Where am I really running to?”<<N>> he thought. <<VAKULA>>“As if everything were already lost. I will try one more way: I will go to the Zaporozhian, Pot-bellied Patsyuk. They say he knows all the devils and will do whatever he wants. I will go, for my soul will have to be lost anyway!”<<N>> At this the devil, who had lain a long time without any movement, began to jump for joy in the sack; but the blacksmith, thinking that he had somehow caught the sack with his hand and made the movement himself, struck the sack with his stout fist and, shaking it on his shoulders, set off to Pot-bellied Patsyuk."
EN[100] = "This Pot-bellied Patsyuk had indeed once been a Zaporozhian; but whether they had driven him out or he had himself run away from the Zaporozhian Host, no one knew. It was long since, ten years, perhaps fifteen, that he had lived in Dikanka. At first he lived like a true Zaporozhian: he did no work, slept three quarters of the day, ate for six mowers, and drank almost a whole pail at one time; though there was room for it, because Patsyuk, for all his small height, was quite hefty in breadth. Besides, the trousers he wore were so wide that however big a step he took, his legs could not be seen at all, and it seemed as if a distillery vat were moving down the street. Perhaps this very thing gave rise to his nickname, Pot-bellied. Only a few days had passed after his arrival in the village when everyone already knew that he was a healer. If anyone was ill with anything, they at once called Patsyuk; and all Patsyuk had to do was whisper a few words, and the ailment was removed as if by hand. If it happened that a hungry gentleman choked on a fishbone, Patsyuk knew how to strike him on the back with his fist so skilfully that the bone went where it ought, causing no harm to the gentleman's throat. Lately he was rarely seen anywhere. The reason for this was perhaps laziness, perhaps also that squeezing through doors became harder for him every year. Then the villagers had to go to him themselves if they had need of him. The blacksmith, not without timidity, opened the door and saw Patsyuk sitting on the floor, Turkish fashion, in front of a small tub on which stood a bowl of dumplings. This bowl stood, as if on purpose, level with his mouth. Without moving a single finger he bent his head slightly to the bowl and slurped the broth, catching the dumplings now and then with his teeth. <<VAKULA>>“No, this one,”<<N>> thought Vakula to himself, <<VAKULA>>“is even lazier than Chub: Chub at least eats with a spoon; and this one won't even lift a hand!”<<N>> Patsyuk was surely deeply occupied with the dumplings, for he seemed not to have noticed the blacksmith's arrival at all, and the blacksmith, hardly setting foot on the threshold, gave him a very low bow."
EN[101] = "<<VAKULA>>“I have come to your honour, Patsyuk!”<<N>> said Vakula, bowing again. The fat Patsyuk raised his head and began again to slurp his dumplings."
EN[102] = "<<VAKULA>>“They say, no offence meant,”<<N>> said the blacksmith, gathering his courage, <<VAKULA>>“I say this not to cause you any insult, that you are somewhat related to the devil.”"
EN[103] = "Having said these words, Vakula was frightened, thinking that he had still spoken too plainly and had softened the strong words too little, and expecting that Patsyuk, seizing the tub together with the bowl, would send it straight at his head, he drew back a little and covered himself with his sleeve so that the hot broth from the dumplings would not splash his face."
EN[104] = "But Patsyuk glanced at him and began again to slurp his dumplings."
EN[105] = "Encouraged, the blacksmith decided to go on: <<VAKULA>>“I have come to you, Patsyuk, may God give you everything, all good things in plenty, bread in proportion!”<<N>> The blacksmith sometimes knew how to slip in a fashionable word; he had become practised in that while still in Poltava, when he was painting the colonel's board fence. <<VAKULA>>“I am to be lost, sinner that I am! Nothing helps in the world! Come what may, I must ask for help from the devil himself. Well, Patsyuk?”<<N>> said the blacksmith, seeing his unchanging silence. <<VAKULA>>“What am I to do?”"
EN[106] = "<<PATSYUK>>“When you need the devil, go to the devil!”<<N>> answered Patsyuk, not raising his eyes to him and going on clearing away the dumplings."
EN[107] = "<<VAKULA>>“That is why I have come to you,”<<N>> answered the blacksmith, making a bow, <<VAKULA>>“I think no one in the world but you knows the way to him.”"
EN[108] = "Patsyuk said not a word, and finished the last dumplings. <<VAKULA>>“Do me the kindness, good man, do not refuse!”<<N>> the blacksmith pressed on. <<VAKULA>>“Pork, or sausages, buckwheat flour, well, linen, millet, or anything else in case of need... as is usual among good people... we will not be stingy; only tell me how, let us say, to get on the road to him?”"
EN[109] = "<<PATSYUK>>“He need not go far who has the devil at his back,”<<N>> said Patsyuk indifferently, without changing his position."
EN[110] = "Vakula fixed his eyes on him as if the explanation of these words were written on his forehead. What is he saying? his look asked silently; and his half-open mouth was ready to swallow the first word like a dumpling. But Patsyuk was silent. Then Vakula noticed that neither dumplings nor tub were before him; instead there stood on the floor two wooden bowls: one was filled with vareniki, the other with sour cream. His thoughts and eyes turned involuntarily to these dishes. <<VAKULA>>“Let us see,”<<N>> he said to himself, <<VAKULA>>“how Patsyuk will eat the vareniki. He surely will not want to bend down to slurp them as he did the dumplings, and it is not possible: a varenik must first be dipped in the sour cream.”<<N>> He had hardly had time to think this when Patsyuk opened his mouth, looked at the vareniki, and opened his mouth wider. At that moment a varenik splashed out of the bowl, plopped into the sour cream, turned over on the other side, leaped up, and landed right in his mouth. Patsyuk ate it and opened his mouth again, and a varenik went the same way again. He took upon himself only the work of chewing and swallowing. <<VAKULA>>“Look, what a marvel!”<<N>> thought the blacksmith, opening his mouth in amazement, and at that very moment noticed that a varenik was climbing into his mouth too and had already smeared his lips with sour cream. Pushing the varenik away and wiping his lips, the blacksmith began to reflect on what wonders there are in the world and what wisdom the unclean spirit can lead a man to, noting at the same time that only Patsyuk could help him. <<VAKULA>>“I will bow to him once more, let him explain it properly... But what the devil! Today is the fasting kutya; and he is eating vareniki, meat-day vareniki! What sort of fool am I, really: standing here and gathering sin! Back!”<<N>> And the pious blacksmith ran headlong out of the cottage."
EN[111] = "But the devil, who sat in the sack and had been rejoicing beforehand, could not bear that such a fine prey should escape his hands. As soon as the blacksmith put down the sack, he jumped out of it and sat astride his neck."
EN[112] = "A chill ran over the blacksmith's skin; frightened and pale, he did not know what to do, and already wanted to cross himself... But the devil, bending his doggy little snout to his right ear, said: <<DEVIL>>“It is I, your friend; I will do everything for a comrade and friend! I will give you as much money as you like,”<<N>> he squeaked into his left ear. <<DEVIL>>“Oksana will be ours today,”<<N>> he whispered, turning his muzzle again to the right ear. The blacksmith stood thinking."
EN[113] = "<<VAKULA>>“Very well,”<<N>> he said at last, <<VAKULA>>“for such a price I am ready to be yours!”"
EN[114] = "The devil clapped his hands and began to gallop with joy on the blacksmith's neck. <<DEVIL>>“Now the blacksmith is caught!”<<N>> he thought to himself. <<DEVIL>>“Now I will pay you back, my dear, for all your paintings and the tall tales told about devils! What will my comrades say now when they learn that the most pious man in the whole village is in my hands?”<<N>> Here the devil laughed for joy, remembering how he would tease the whole tailed tribe in hell, how the lame devil, considered the first among them for inventions, would rage."
EN[115] = "<<DEVIL>>“Well, Vakula!”<<N>> squeaked the devil, still not getting down from his neck, as if afraid that he would run away. <<DEVIL>>“You know that nothing is done without a contract.”"
EN[116] = "<<VAKULA>>“I am ready!”<<N>> said the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“I hear that with you they sign in blood; wait, I will get a nail out of my pocket!”<<N>> Here he put his hand behind him, and grab! he had the devil by the tail."
EN[117] = "<<DEVIL>>“Look what a joker!”<<N>> cried the devil, laughing. <<DEVIL>>“Well, enough, enough of this mischief!”"
EN[118] = "<<VAKULA>>“Wait, my dear!”<<N>> cried the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“And how does this strike you?”<<N>> At these words he made the sign of the cross, and the devil became as quiet as a lamb. <<VAKULA>>“Wait, then,”<<N>> he said, dragging him down to the ground by the tail, <<VAKULA>>“you will learn from me to prompt good people and honest Christians to sin.”<<N>> Then the blacksmith, without letting go of the tail, leaped astride him and raised his hand for the sign of the cross."
EN[119] = "<<DEVIL>>“Have mercy, Vakula!”<<N>> groaned the devil pitifully. <<DEVIL>>“Everything you need, I will do; only let my soul go to repentance: do not lay the terrible cross on me!”"
EN[120] = "<<VAKULA>>“Ah, so that is the voice you sing in, accursed German! Now I know what to do. Carry me on yourself this very instant! Do you hear, carry me like a bird!”"
EN[121] = "<<DEVIL>>“Where?”<<N>> said the sorrowful devil."
EN[122] = "<<VAKULA>>“To Petersburg, straight to the Empress!”<<N>> And the blacksmith went numb with fear, feeling himself rise into the air."
